\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces The MLE-fit exponential truncation parameter $c$ partially captures the distribution tails, and performs well for avalanche size at higher $L$. Select lattice sizes $L = 5, 30, 100$ are depicted - the fits for all simulated lattice sizes are shown in Appendix Figure \ref {fig:APPENDIX_exp_powerlaw_mle_viz}. MLEs are drawn as black points with a connecting line drawn for illustration purposes. The left column shows avalanche size, the centre column shows avalanche area, and the right column shows avalanche $L_1$ distance. The dashed vertical lines show the fixed parameter $x_{max}$.}}{16}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:powerlaw_exp_fitted_mle}{{4.3}{16}{The MLE-fit exponential truncation parameter $c$ partially captures the distribution tails, and performs well for avalanche size at higher $L$. Select lattice sizes $L = 5, 30, 100$ are depicted - the fits for all simulated lattice sizes are shown in Appendix Figure \ref {fig:APPENDIX_exp_powerlaw_mle_viz}. MLEs are drawn as black points with a connecting line drawn for illustration purposes. The left column shows avalanche size, the centre column shows avalanche area, and the right column shows avalanche $L_1$ distance. The dashed vertical lines show the fixed parameter $x_{max}$}{figure.caption.12}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Unpooled MLE estimates of the parameters (points) vs pooled MLE parameter estimates (dashed lines). If the pmf form and the finite size scaling hypothesis are appropriate, we expect the dashed line to approximate the trend of the points. Based on the above, the model is not quite appropriate, and we need to consider an additional model for the pmf.}}{17}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pooled_unpooled_exp_comparison}{{4.4}{17}{Unpooled MLE estimates of the parameters (points) vs pooled MLE parameter estimates (dashed lines). If the pmf form and the finite size scaling hypothesis are appropriate, we expect the dashed line to approximate the trend of the points. Based on the above, the model is not quite appropriate, and we need to consider an additional model for the pmf}{figure.caption.13}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Pooled MLE coefficients for finite size scaling assuming the exponentially truncated power law distribution. We expect $\hat  {D}_s, \hat  {D}_a \approx 2$, $\hat  {D}_d \approx 1$, and $\hat  {\tau }_s, \hat  {\tau }_a \approx 1$, so the results seem reasonable.}}{17}{figure.caption.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:pooled_powerlaw_MLEs}{{4.2}{17}{Pooled MLE coefficients for finite size scaling assuming the exponentially truncated power law distribution. We expect $\hat {D}_s, \hat {D}_a \approx 2$, $\hat {D}_d \approx 1$, and $\hat {\tau }_s, \hat {\tau }_a \approx 1$, so the results seem reasonable}{figure.caption.13}{}}
