\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Visualisation of the stretched exponentially truncated power law probability mass function on log-log axes under different parameter combinations. Each point represents a discrete value and corresponding probability mass, with a line connecting sequential points for illustration purposes. The left three plots are similar to Figure \ref {fig:exp_powerlaw_params} as they keep $\beta = 1$. The right plot shows the effect of increasing $\beta $ while keeping $\tau $, $x_{max}$, and $c$ constant, which results in steeper decay.The orange line is identical in all plots, corresponding to parameters $\tau = 0.5$, $x_{max} = 100$, $c = 50$, and $\beta = 1$.}}{19}{figure.caption.15}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:stretched_powerlaw_params}{{4.6}{19}{Visualisation of the stretched exponentially truncated power law probability mass function on log-log axes under different parameter combinations. Each point represents a discrete value and corresponding probability mass, with a line connecting sequential points for illustration purposes. The left three plots are similar to Figure \ref {fig:exp_powerlaw_params} as they keep $\beta = 1$. The right plot shows the effect of increasing $\beta $ while keeping $\tau $, $x_{max}$, and $c$ constant, which results in steeper decay.The orange line is identical in all plots, corresponding to parameters $\tau = 0.5$, $x_{max} = 100$, $c = 50$, and $\beta = 1$}{figure.caption.15}{}}
