\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces A comparison of the unpooled estimates of the parameters (points) with the pooled parameter estimates (dashed lines). If the pmf form and the finite size scaling hypothesis are appropriate, we expect the dashed line to approximate the trend of the points. Based on the above, the pooled model for avalanche area is reasonable, while evidence suggests avalanche size and length may have an $L-$dependent stretching parameter $\beta _s$. The power law coefficient for length also appears to be $L-$dependent.}}{21}{figure.caption.18}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:stretched_pooled_unpooled_exp_comparison}{{4.8}{21}{A comparison of the unpooled estimates of the parameters (points) with the pooled parameter estimates (dashed lines). If the pmf form and the finite size scaling hypothesis are appropriate, we expect the dashed line to approximate the trend of the points. Based on the above, the pooled model for avalanche area is reasonable, while evidence suggests avalanche size and length may have an $L-$dependent stretching parameter $\beta _s$. The power law coefficient for length also appears to be $L-$dependent}{figure.caption.18}{}}
